│ Task: OWASP A10 Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions: behaviour under malformed, oversized,       │
│ wrong-type, boundary and empty input, including whether failures open rather than close access.    │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Probe boundaries and malformed input                                                        │
│ Objective: Find where the application stops handling input gracefully.                             │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. For representative endpoints, submit wrong types, empty values, missing required fields,      │
│ extra unexpected fields, boundary numbers, and structurally malformed bodies.                      │
│   2. Try oversized values within reason - enough to test a limit, not enough to exhaust resources. │
│ Do not pursue denial of service.                                                                   │
│   3. Record unhandled exceptions, stack traces, inconsistent status codes, and any response that   │
│ discloses internal structure.                                                                      │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Does failure open or close?                                                                 │
│ Objective: The security-relevant question.                                                         │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Determine whether an error path skips a control - whether a malformed request reaches a       │
│ handler that a well-formed one could not, or whether a failed check results in access rather than  │
│ denial.                                                                                            │
│   2. Where an error reveals interpreter or framework detail, record it as an oracle for injection  │
│ and integrity testing.                                                                             │
│   3. Run the control test: the same request well-formed, reported alongside.                       │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Verify before reporting                                                                     │
│ Objective: Apply the same evidence standard to every finding, so results are comparable across     │
│ categories.                                                                                        │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. PROOF DISCIPLINE - label every finding DEMONSTRATED (you executed it and the response proves  │
│ it, quote that response), INFERRED (follows from evidence but was not executed), or UNVERIFIED     │
│ (suspected from a version, component or code pattern only). Identifying software is never a        │
│ demonstration; never write 'confirmed' without having executed something.                          │
│   2. RUN THE CONTROL TEST. Before claiming any access or bypass, repeat the request WITHOUT the    │
│ credential, token, header or material you believe is responsible. If the result is the same, you   │
│ have proven nothing - the resource was already reachable. State the control result alongside the   │
│ finding. This single omission is the most common source of false critical findings.                │
│   3. BUDGET DISCIPLINE - rank leads by evidence strength (material in hand > observed behaviour >  │
│ suspicion from a version string) and give each a step budget. STOP RULE: about 8-10 consecutive    │
│ steps on one lead with no new evidence means move on, recording what you tried.                    │
│   4. Record findings to notes as you go, with the endpoint, the request, the response and the      │
│ control result, so later phases and the report can use them.                                       │
│   5. Stay non-destructive: prefer read-only proof, do not modify or delete data, do not lock       │
│ accounts, and stop once the point is demonstrated.                                                 │
│                                                                                                    │
│                                                                                                    │
